WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.783
Why test a message before it goes public?

00:00:02.783 --> 00:00:06.957
Because the meaning a team intends is not always the meaning a

00:00:06.957 --> 00:00:08.000
reader takes away.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.840
Show the actual draft to people it is meant to reach.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:18.120
Before explaining it, ask them to describe what it means in their

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:19.000
own words.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Then ask what they would do next.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.143
A message can sound appealing while leaving the date, location or required

00:00:27.143 --> 00:00:28.000
action unclear.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:33.333
Imagine a notice that says, expanded access begins next month.

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:39.200
Does that mean longer hours, a new location, or different services?

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:44.000
This is an illustrative example, not a client finding.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.400
Separate confusion from disagreement.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:53.000
Revise missing information, and test again when the decision warrants it.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:59.000
Qualitative feedback explains interpretations; it is not a population-wide vote.

